ions are possible based on a stress on dual motions, one forward in calendar time toward disillusion and one backward toward the redemptive recovery of time, in each work."}}, "retrieval_6": {"pred": "Betrayal is a genre of fiction that focuses on the theme of betrayal and its consequences.", "score": 3.1388355925595155, "ctx": {"title": "Betrayal", "text": "Betrayal\n\nBetrayal is the breaking or violation of a presumptive contract, trust, or confidence that produces moral and psychological conflict within a relationship amongst individuals, between organizations or between individuals and organizations. Often betrayal is the act of supporting a rival group, or it is a complete break from previously decided upon or presumed norms by one party from the others. Someone who betrays others is commonly called a traitor or betrayer. Betrayal is also a commonly used literary element, also used in other fiction like films and TV series, and is often associated with or used as a plot twist."}}, "retrieval_7": {"pred": "Betrayal is a novel by Danielle Steel.", "score": 3.2308309434601528, "ctx": {"title": "Betrayal (Steel novel)", "text": "Betrayal (Steel novel)\n\nBetrayal is a novel by Danielle Steel, published by Delacorte Press in July 2012. The book is Steel's eighty-sixth novel, and (including non-fiction and children's books) her 104th book overall. It reached number 3 in the New York Times hardback fiction bestsellers chart. It is also available in audio book, read by Renee Raudman."}}, "retrieval_8": {"pred": "Betrayal is a board game genre.", "score": 2.626270366604516, "ctx": {"title": "Betrayal at House on the Hill", "text": "Betrayal at House on the Hill\n\nBetrayal at House on the Hill is a board game published by Avalon Hill in 2004, designed by Bruce Glassco and developed by Rob Daviau, Bill McQuillan, Mike Selinker, and Teeuwynn Woodruff. Players all begin as allies exploring a haunted house filled with dangers, traps, items, and omens. As players journey to